# Accessibility

Accessibility is a functional requirement.

## Semantic HTML

Prefer native elements:

- button
- a
- nav
- main
- header
- footer
- form
- label
- input
- select
- textarea

Do not recreate native semantics unnecessarily.

## Keyboard

All interactive functionality must be usable with keyboard input.

Ensure:

- logical tab order
- visible focus
- keyboard activation
- no keyboard traps

## Focus

Do not remove focus indicators without providing a better equivalent.

After dialogs/navigation/state changes, manage focus intentionally when necessary.

## Labels

Inputs need meaningful accessible labels.

Do not rely on placeholder text as the only label.

## Images

Provide useful alternative text when an image conveys information.

Decorative images should not create unnecessary screen-reader noise.

## Color

Do not communicate information through color alone.

## Motion

Respect reduced-motion preferences.

## ARIA

Use ARIA when native HTML cannot express the required semantics.

Do not add ARIA unnecessarily.

Prefer correct HTML over excessive ARIA.

## Interactive elements

Do not use:

`<div onClick>`

when a native button or link is appropriate.

## Dialogs

Dialogs must have:

- accessible name
- appropriate focus management
- keyboard dismissal where appropriate
- sensible focus return

## Testing

When appropriate, verify:

- keyboard navigation
- focus states
- semantic structure
- screen-reader labeling
- contrast